<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 1</title></head><body><h1>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 1</h1><p>Paul Banyard — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 1)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>03-04 Performance Review</p><p>Plaud file id: d9c1bd41ef624d6622ac460fee09f94f</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance and Pipeline Review for Paul Banyard</p><p>This document summarises a performance review meeting between Bob Duncan (Manager), Paul Banyard (Advisor), and Sean McLennan (Support). The session focused on reviewing past actions, analysing Paul&#x27;s current sales pipeline—specifically his high conversion rates despite lead contact challenges—and evaluating key performance metrics against quarterly targets. Strategic discussions highlighted the significant upcoming remortgage pipeline and the need to focus on high-quality Edinburgh leads, culminating in specific action items for lead management, referral generation, and administrative support for Paul&#x27;s current application volume.</p><p>## Review of Previous Actions and Current Lead Status</p><p>The meeting commenced with a review of action items from the previous session. Bob Duncan confirmed that Paul Banyard had successfully actioned his primary tasks: reviewing his debts and clearing his overdue &quot;top&quot; workflows. Paul clarified that the majority of his workflows had been actively worked, with some merely reviewed to ascertain client status before being moved forward.</p><p>Attention then shifted to lead management and data hygiene. Sean McLennan reported significant progress in cleaning up lead statuses, having conducted a &quot;big clear out&quot; the previous day. He noted that he has been processing these in large tranches approximately once a week over the last month and feels close to being completely up to date. Bob emphasised the critical nature of this task as the team prepares to migrate to a new platform. He highlighted that accurate lead statuses are essential because the new system will automate certain chasing tasks via email journeys, reducing the manual call load.</p><p>Bob also introduced a new collaborative approach for &quot;keeping in touch&quot; calls, suggesting that Paul and Sean could alternate these tasks to ensure consistent client contact without overburdening one individual. The consensus was that maintaining these workflows is vital for managing the pipeline effectively.</p><p>## Sales Pipeline Analysis: Leads, DIPs, and Applications</p><p>A detailed examination of Paul&#x27;s sales pipeline revealed positive momentum despite some inherent challenges with lead contactability.</p><p>*   **Lead Volume:** February saw a total of 34 leads.</p><p>*   **Lead Breakdown:** The breakdown included 13 &quot;NPW&quot; (Not Proceeding With), 7 &quot;keeping in contact,&quot; and 1 at the &quot;DIP&quot; (Decision in Principle) stage.</p><p>*   **Challenges:** The largest category remained &quot;unable to contact,&quot; a common issue across the advisory team.</p><p>*   **Immediate Conversion:** Encouragingly, 5 cases from the 34 February leads were processed immediately, demonstrating strong conversion when contact is made.</p><p>The review of specific pipeline stages showed 4 leads at the &quot;appointment booked&quot; stage and 5 at &quot;appointment pending.&quot; Bob urged Paul to focus on increasing the number of clients at the DIP stage, noting that with the new platform, the requirement for documents to obtain a DIP has been removed. This change lowers the barrier to getting a commitment in principle, which Bob views as crucial for locking clients in.</p><p>Currently, Paul&#x27;s pipeline business stands at £183,000. Bob noted it was &quot;brilliant&quot; that all of Paul&#x27;s active mortgage applications are currently at the offer stage. Conversely, there are only two protection cases in underwriting. One of these is a complex, ongoing case involving a client named Jasmine, where previous policies were postponed. Her partner, David Lee, has policies currently with underwriting. Sean McLennan noted that insurer Service Level Agreements (SLAs) are currently poor (&quot;ridiculous&quot;), but agreed to actively chase underwriting and GP reports to move the case forward.</p><p>## Performance Metrics and KPI Evaluation</p><p>The session moved to a quantitative analysis of Paul&#x27;s financial performance and Key Performance Indicators (KPIs).</p><p>*   **Projected Income:** Paul is projected to be well into bonus territory for March and April based on banked business. The current breakdown includes £10,000 in insurance income due, £1,600 in predicted admin fees, and £7,000 in mortgage income.</p><p>*   **Income Timing:** Bob clarified that projected income figures are derived from written business that has moved past the initial &quot;add&quot; stage. He stressed the importance of accurate data entry regarding completion or &quot;on-risk&quot; dates, as these drive the financial projections. Specifically, the cases for David Lee and Jasmine Hepburn lacked on-risk dates, meaning their income was not yet assigned to a specific month.</p><p>*   **Quarterly Targets:** Paul is ahead of his target, having agreed to deliver £45,000 in written business for the quarter. He has already banked £10,000 in February, with a total banked income sitting around £30,000–£35,000 so far.</p><p>*   **Conversion Rates:** Paul&#x27;s overall conversion rate for Q3 was a strong 66%. For Q4, excluding clients who were physically unable to be contacted, his conversion rate stands at 54%. This is a significant positive, indicating that when Paul actually speaks to a client, he converts more than half of them into business.</p><p>*   **Average Metrics:** His average insurance premium for Q4 is £160 (well above the aim of £75), with an average case size just under £2,000. However, his admin fee penetration for Q4 is currently at 56%, which Bob noted was lower than usual and worth monitoring to ensure he isn&#x27;t &quot;underselling&quot; his service.</p><p>## Future Pipeline and Strategic Growth Opportunities</p><p>Discussion then turned to future growth strategies, particularly focusing on the upcoming remortgage pipeline and lead quality.</p><p>*   **Remortgage Opportunities:** A review of the back book revealed a robust pipeline for the next financial year, with significant activity expected in June, September, and December. Bob estimated this remortgage business represents just shy of £50,000 in immediate income, suggesting a potentially &quot;great year&quot; ahead.</p><p>*   **Strategic Focus:** To maximise efficiency, Paul expressed a preference for focusing on the Edinburgh market. He argued that his local knowledge allows him to add more value to these clients compared to leads from the &quot;CE team,&quot; which often take longer to convert.</p><p>*   **Referral Strategy:** Bob identified a key growth opportunity in client referrals. Data showed Paul is just behind the top performer, Karen, in business generated from recommendations, despite not actively pushing for them. Paul admitted he has relied on organic referrals from happy clients. Bob encouraged him to build a habit of explicitly asking for referrals at key &quot;good news&quot; moments, such as mortgage offer and completion, to secure &quot;easy win&quot; business.</p><p>*   **Lead Volume Targets:** The operational goal is to secure three quality appointments per day. Bob advised Paul to ensure his diary has space for these appointments, noting that currently, bookings are often made 24 hours in advance.</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>### **@Paul Banyard**</p><p>- [ ] Maintain and manage the DIPs; aim to grow the number of clients at DIP stage. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Develop a consistent habit of asking for client referrals at key milestones (offer/completion). - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Maintain workflows by updating them daily, including making phone calls and sending emails. - [Daily]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure all dates (on-risk, completion, exchange) are accurate throughout the system to ensure correct income projection. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Monitor lead sources and formally request a focus on Edinburgh leads over CE team leads if desired. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure fact finds are accurate and illustrations are on file before handing over to Sean for keying. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Update the lead status for client Oliver Humphreys. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Secure an on-risk date for the David Lee and Jasmine Hepburn protection policies. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Continue monitoring the Brianna renewal case regarding the EWS1 form. - [TBD]</p><p>### **@Sean McLennan**</p><p>- [ ] Call underwriting and GPs to proactively push the Jasmine Hepburn/David Lee protection case. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Key Paul Banyard’s five outstanding applications for the week (requires 3 years address history and credit card balances). - [This Week]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Paul Banyard</p><p>**Review date:** 2026-03-04</p><p>**Content creation date:** 2026-03-04 11:46:28</p><p>---</p><p>## 1. Follow-Up From Previous Meeting</p><p>### Actions previously agreed</p><p>- Review and clear **debts**.</p><p>- Clear **overdue tasks/workflows**.</p><p>- Clean up **lead statuses** (primarily **Sean** to support).</p><p>- Review/drive **DIPs**.</p><p>- Update a **couple of mortgage/insurance cases** in CRM.</p><p>### Completed vs outstanding</p><p>- **Completed (Paul):**</p><p>  - Debts reviewed/cleared.</p><p>  - Overdue tasks/workflows cleared.</p><p>- **Partially completed (Paul):**</p><p>  - Workflow handling: majority worked, but some items were **moved forward without full client action logged** (after checking client position).</p><p>- **Completed (Sean):**</p><p>  - Lead status clear-out completed on **2026-03-03** (bulk update approach).</p><p>- **Outstanding / not evidenced as completed:**</p><p>  - **DIP progression uplift** (explicitly still low; expectation to rise week-on-week).</p><p>  - Updates to the **mortgage/insurance cases in CRM** were discussed as required actions, but no completion evidence captured in the review content.</p><p>### Reasons for delay / issues</p><p>- Some workflows progressed without full activity/notes logged, creating **status movement without evidenced client action**.</p><p>### Repeated missed actions / ongoing blockers</p><p>- Recurring theme across the review set: **work completed but CRM not updated in real time** (e.g., workflow moves without notes; DIP completed but status not updated).</p><p>### Behaviour patterns</p><p>- **Delivery is strong once clients are engaged**, but performance is constrained by:</p><p>  - **CRM lag** (statuses/dates/notes not updated promptly).</p><p>  - **Follow-up evidencing** (progression recorded without clear audit trail).</p><p>### Risk created by inaction</p><p>- If workflows/statuses are advanced without notes/outcomes, **pipeline reporting becomes unreliable**, blockers (e.g., missing documents) become less visible, and cases can drift unnoticed—higher risk ahead of **platform** process changes.</p><p>### Trend insight</p><p>- **Improving** — overdue items cleared and lead status hygiene improving due to Sean’s cadence; however, **CRM note quality** remains a recurring risk.</p><p>---</p><p>## 2. Leads Performance Review (With CRM Discipline)</p><p>### A. Leads — Last Month (February)</p><p>#### Current position (February)</p><p>- **34 leads total**.</p><p>- Status distribution referenced:</p><p>  - **13** marked **NPWS**</p><p>  - **7** marked **Keeping in Contact**</p><p>  - **1** at **DIP stage**</p><p>  - Largest category referenced: **Unable to Contact** (count not stated)</p><p>- **5 cases** attributed to February leads converting quickly into written cases.</p><p>#### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- No February comparative totals provided vs prior period.</p><p>#### What’s working well</p><p>- February lead conversations generated **5 written cases quickly**.</p><p>- Lead pipeline hygiene improving due to **Sean’s** structured clean-outs (including bulk update on **2026-03-03**).</p><p>#### What’s not working well</p><p>- High “Unable to Contact” presence (conversion drag).</p><p>- CRM categorisation noise:</p><p>  - “**Appointment Missed**” and “**NPW duplicate lead**” statuses cluttering views and affecting reporting clarity.</p><p>#### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Reporting accuracy risk if lead statuses are not consistently maintained—especially important as journeys/task-routing become more dependent on CRM.</p><p>- Conversion risk if “Unable to Contact” leads are not diarised and re-attempted with a clear cadence (cadence not evidenced in notes presented).</p><p>#### Actions required</p><p>- Continue weekly hygiene of lead statuses and removal/merging of duplicates where applicable.</p><p>- Increase the proportion of leads progressed to **DIP/AIP** (explicit expectation: **DIP count should rise week-on-week** as a personal KPI).</p><p>#### Action owner</p><p>- Lead status hygiene / duplicates clean-up: **Admin/Other (Sean)**</p><p>- DIP progression focus: **Adviser (Paul)**</p><p>#### Trend insight</p><p>- **Improving** — lead list closer to “up to date” due to shared ownership; **DIP volume remains the limiting factor**.</p><p>---</p><p>### Lead conversion &amp; contactability (overall leads view)</p><p>#### Current position</p><p>- Total leads shown: **359** (noted as “take with a pinch of salt” due to contactability).</p><p>- **66%** categorised as effectively **unable to progress** (unable to contact / never spoken to / no-show).</p><p>- Conversion:</p><p>  - **14%** across all leads (including uncontactables)</p><p>  - **54%** when excluding “unable to contact” style outcomes</p><p>- Outcomes referenced:</p><p>  - **52 applications**</p><p>  - **24 completed**</p><p>#### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- Not available in the review content.</p><p>#### What’s working well</p><p>- When clients are engaged, conversion is strong (**54%** excluding uncontactables).</p><p>#### What’s not working well</p><p>- Contactability/no-show volume (**66%**) suppresses overall performance and obscures true conversion capability.</p><p>- Diary management constraint: appointments typically booked **~24 hours in advance**, limiting ability to absorb higher lead volumes.</p><p>#### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Revenue leakage from uncontacted/unattended leads.</p><p>- Capacity risk as application workload increases, reducing lead follow-up time.</p><p>#### Actions required</p><p>- Drive towards **3 quality appointments per day** (quality and attendance-focused).</p><p>- Maintain workflows and touchpoints to avoid losing active house-hunters.</p><p>#### Action owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Paul)** + **Other (booking team referenced)**</p><p>#### Trend insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — conversion is strong once spoken to, but overall utilisation is constrained by **contactability + appointment control**.</p><p>---</p><p>## 3. “Where Are My Leads?”</p><p>### Current position</p><p>- Paul referenced **13 customers in pipeline** in the context of pushing **DIPs**.</p><p>- Direction given: use updated guidance (documents not required for AIP/DIP process as referenced) to drive faster commitment via **DIP/AIP**.</p><p>### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- No prior DIP/pipeline counts provided.</p><p>### What’s working well</p><p>- Shared follow-up model emerging between **Paul and Sean** (planned alternating “keeping in touch” calls with routed tasks).</p><p>### What’s not working well</p><p>- **DIP volume low** relative to pipeline size (explicitly called out).</p><p>### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Without increasing DIP/AIP capture, commitment is weaker and fallout risk increases (clients drift to other brokers/lenders).</p><p>### Actions required</p><p>- Make DIP progression a weekly priority: move appropriate “Keeping in Contact” leads to **DIP/AIP** and log clear next steps.</p><p>### Action owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Paul)**</p><p>### Trend insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — explicit push required to lift DIP/AIP capture; current pipeline size not reflected in DIP volume.</p><p>---</p><p>## 4. Applications in Progress</p><p>### Current position</p><p>- CRM indicates pipeline business of **£183,000**; confirmed during the review.</p><p>- Pipeline appears to be **fully at offer** (“everything’s offer”).</p><p>### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- No prior pipeline value/stage mix provided.</p><p>### What’s working well</p><p>- Strong progression discipline to offer stage across current pipeline.</p><p>- Ability to scale with admin support: **Sean** can key applications quickly when the **fact-find is correct** and **illustration is on file**.</p><p>### What’s not working well</p><p>- Document delays slowing progression:</p><p>  - **Lloyd Dunlop** — waiting on documents.</p><p>  - **Mendon Dombrook** — offer accepted, still requires documents to progress.</p><p>- Time pressure: Paul estimated **30–45 minutes per application** plus notes/reports, compressing time for lead engagement and proactive activity.</p><p>### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Slippage risk and pipeline overstatement where documents are missing (Lloyd Dunlop, Mendon Dombrook).</p><p>- Application accuracy risk if packs are incomplete (common blockers cited: missing **3-year address history** and **credit card balances**), causing rework and delays.</p><p>- Workflow/note gaps risk if cases are moved without recorded outcomes.</p><p>### Actions required (case-level)</p><p>- **Lloyd Dunlop**: chase/secure outstanding documents; update CRM blockers/next steps.</p><p>- **Mendon Dombrook**: request/track documents; progress to next stage and update CRM once received.</p><p>- **Raymond Hamilton (~£100k remortgage)**: progress application and keep CRM stage accurate.</p><p>- **Jacob &amp; Siobhan Duncan (Renewal)**: confirm appointment status and progress renewal now documents are received.</p><p>- **Brianna (Renewal / remortgage vs product transfer)**: review client email re: EWS1 uncertainty; decide route; log next steps.</p><p>- **Oliver Humphreys**: DIP completed but lead status not updated; update CRM status/ownership and next steps.</p><p>- **Somer Hilo**:</p><p>  - **Lender:** Santander</p><p>  - Provide complete pack so Sean can key (fact-find + illustration + 3-year address history + liabilities).</p><p>### Action owner</p><p>- Adviser tasks / client chasing / CRM stages: **Adviser (Paul)**</p><p>- Keying once pack complete: **Admin (Sean)**</p><p>### Trend insight</p><p>- **Improving** — strong “at offer” position and admin leverage available; constrained by **document lag** and **pack completeness/CRM evidencing**.</p><p>---</p><p>## 5. Protection in Progress</p><p>### Current position</p><p>- A large protection case placed **on risk** with premium “**£270-something**” per month.</p><p>- **Jasmine (and partner)**: protection remains delayed:</p><p>  - Hers postponed; his in referral/underwriting.</p><p>  - History of postponements since **2024** protection review (initial budget **£150/month**).</p><p>- Additional protection workflow issue impacting revenue forecasting:</p><p>  - Missing **on-risk dates** for **David Lee** and **Jasmine Hepburn** in projected income view (cases show in totals but do not allocate to a month).</p><p>### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- No prior underwriting count or on-risk date completeness comparison provided.</p><p>### What’s working well</p><p>- Material premium successfully moved to on-risk (revenue contribution).</p><p>- Clear plan to escalate blocked protection progress using Business Manager where applicable.</p><p>### What’s not working well</p><p>- Ongoing underwriting/postponement cycle on Jasmine case consuming time and risking client fatigue.</p><p>- Forecast accuracy undermined by missing on-risk dates (**David Lee**, **Jasmine Hepburn**).</p><p>### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Underwriting/provider SLA delays described as “ridiculous”, increasing time-to-risk and lapse risk.</p><p>- Client risk where a client is in property and underinsured (used as rationale for escalation on an outstanding insurance case; client name not stated).</p><p>- Revenue timing risk: missing/incorrect dates misstate month-by-month bonus expectations and can hide slippage into later months.</p><p>### Actions required</p><p>- **Jasmine (protection):** Sean to call underwriting/provider/GPS to push referral forward; update workflow outcome.</p><p>- **Outstanding insurance case (name not stated):** escalate via **Business Manager** using “client in property/underinsured” urgency.</p><p>- Add **on-risk dates** for:</p><p>  - **David Lee**</p><p>  - **Jasmine Hepburn**</p><p>- Audit other written cases to ensure **exchange/completion/on-risk** dates are present and realistic.</p><p>### Action owner</p><p>- Underwriting chase: **Admin/Other (Sean)**</p><p>- Escalation via Business Manager: **Adviser (Paul) / Other (Business Manager)**</p><p>- CRM date hygiene: **Adviser (Paul)**</p><p>### Trend insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — underwriting/SLA delays and CRM date gaps are limiting progress and forecast reliability.</p><p>---</p><p>## 7. Banked Business — This Month (data available: February only)</p><p>### Current position</p><p>- **Banked income (February): ~£10,000**.</p><p>- **Total banked income so far (stated): ~£30,000–£35,000**.</p><p>### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>### What’s working well</p><p>- Strong February outcome described as “fantastic”, supporting momentum into March/April.</p><p>### What’s not working well</p><p>- No month-to-date banked figure for March provided in the review content.</p><p>### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Banked performance and projections can be misread if CRM dates (exchange/completion/on-risk) are missing or inaccurate.</p><p>### Actions required</p><p>- Ensure CRM dates are complete and realistic so banked/projection timing reflects actual expected revenue month.</p><p>### Action owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Paul)**</p><p>### Trend insight</p><p>- **Improving** — strong recent banked performance; reliability depends on date discipline.</p><p>---</p><p>## 8. Renewal / Remortgage Income</p><p>### Current position</p><p>- Latest renewal end dates mentioned: **30 June**.</p><p>- Remortgage pipeline timing noted:</p><p>  - **6** due in **March**</p><p>  - **1** due in **April**</p><p>- Remortgage income estimate shown: **just under £50,000**.</p><p>- Identified strong conversation windows ahead: **June, September, December**.</p><p>### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>### What’s working well</p><p>- Visibility of future remortgage value (~**£50k**) provides a base for next year.</p><p>- Awareness of timing and seasonality patterns (June→Sept, Sept→Dec, Dec→March).</p><p>### What’s not working well</p><p>- Risk of deferring activity (especially on near-term due items) without evidenced workflow touchpoints.</p><p>### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Pipeline drift risk if contact cadence drops during busy periods.</p><p>- Forecast timing risk if CRM dates are inaccurate.</p><p>### Actions required</p><p>- Maintain renewal/remortgage workflow touchpoints so June/September/December cohorts remain active.</p><p>- Ensure all remortgage pipeline items have realistic **review/contact dates** and **anticipated completion** recorded.</p><p>### Action owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Paul)**</p><p>### Trend insight</p><p>- **Improving** — opportunity identified and quantified; execution depends on sustained workflow discipline.</p><p>---</p><p>## 10. Projected Income / Pipeline Timing (CRM date discipline)</p><p>### Current position</p><p>- **March–April:** projected income view indicates Paul is **“well into bonus territory”** (where dates are correctly set).</p><p>- Projection breakdown shown:</p><p>  - **Insurance income due:** ~**£10,000**</p><p>  - **Admin fees predicted:** ~**£1,600**</p><p>  - **Mortgage income:** ~**£7,000**</p><p>- Missing on-risk dates causing unallocated monthly projection:</p><p>  - **David Lee** — no on-risk date</p><p>  - **Jasmine Hepburn** — no on-risk date</p><p>### Vs last week / last month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>### What’s working well</p><p>- Written business only appears once **product added + report issued**, supporting more accurate projections when maintained properly.</p><p>- Awareness that incorrect dates can falsely inflate short-term projection.</p><p>### What’s not working well</p><p>- Date completeness gap: missing **on-risk dates** undermines forecast accuracy and month-by-month planning.</p><p>### Risks / blockers</p><p>- Revenue timing risk: missing/incorrect dates can misstate bonus expectations and hide slippage into later months/financial year.</p><p>- Reporting risk: projections unreliable if exchange/completion/on-risk dates are not maintained.</p><p>### Actions required</p><p>- Add **on-risk dates** for **David Lee** and **Jasmine Hepburn**.</p><p>- Audit other written cases for complete and realistic **exchange/completion/on-risk** dates.</p><p>### Action owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Paul)**</p><p>### Trend insight</p><p>- **Improving** — but forecast reliability remains constrained by missing CRM dates.</p><p>---</p><p>## 11. Overall Performance Diagnosis (performance data available)</p><p>### Adviser strengths this week</p><p>- Strong conversion once clients are engaged (**54%** excluding uncontactables).</p><p>- Strong pipeline position at offer (**£183,000**, “everything’s offer”).</p><p>- Positive revenue momentum: **~£10k** banked in February; **~£30–35k** banked total stated.</p><p>- Client recommendation performance strong: approx. **£30,000 written business this year** from Paul’s own client network; **~£1,000 behind Karen** in written business from client recommendations.</p><p>### Main risks</p><p>- Lead contactability/no-show volume remains high (**66%** uncontactable/no-show), suppressing overall conversion.</p><p>- CRM reliability risks:</p><p>  - Work progressed without sufficient notes/audit trail.</p><p>  - Missing on-risk dates (**David Lee**, **Jasmine Hepburn**).</p><p>  - Status not updated after activity (**Oliver Humphreys** DIP done but status not updated).</p><p>- Document-driven slippage risk on named cases (**Lloyd Dunlop**, **Mendon Dombrook**).</p><p>- Margin/fee discipline risk: **Admin fee rate down in Q4 (56%) vs 77% YTD**.</p><p>### Behaviour patterns / accountability concerns</p><p>- Recurring CRM pattern: activity occurs, but **status/dates/notes not updated immediately**, creating ownership gaps and reporting noise.</p><p>### Where management support is required</p><p>- Booking/lead source support to consistently achieve **3 quality appointments/day**.</p><p>- Escalation routes (e.g., **Business Manager**) to progress blocked protection where urgency applies.</p><p>---</p><p>## 12. Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Confirm appointment status and progress renewal case now documents received | Jacob &amp; Siobhan Duncan | Paul (Adviser) | 2026-03-04 | Renewal delay; potential lapse/retention loss |</p><p>| Review client email, decide route (remortgage vs PT) and log CRM next steps | Brianna | Paul (Adviser) | 2026-03-04 | Case drift; incorrect route; loss of momentum |</p><p>| Chase underwriting/provider/GPS on referral and update workflow outcome | Jasmine (Protection) | Sean (Admin/Other) | 2026-03-04 | Underwriting stalls; increased lapse risk due to long delays |</p><p>| Add missing on-risk dates (use realistic projected completion and revise when confirmed) | David Lee; Jasmine Hepburn | Paul (Adviser) | 2026-03-05 | Monthly projections unreliable; income timing/bonus expectations distorted |</p><p>| Update CRM lead status + next steps after DIP completed (ensure ownership and follow-up task logged) | Oliver Humphreys | Paul (Adviser) | 2026-03-05 | Ownership/reporting gaps; missed follow-ups; pipeline visibility reduced |</p><p>| Escalate outstanding insurance case via Business Manager using “client in property/underinsured” urgency | Protection case (name not stated) | Paul (Adviser) / Business Manager (Other) | 2026-03-06 | Client underinsured; protection revenue slippage; prolonged case duration |</p><p>| Review Q4 cases to confirm admin fees applied consistently | Fees / revenue quality | Paul (Adviser) | 2026-03-11 | Margin erosion; inconsistent charging practice |</p><p>| Drive towards 3 quality appointments per day; protect diary capacity and reduce unproductive booking | Lead handling / diary | Paul (Adviser) + Booking team (Other) | 2026-03-11 | Lead leakage; reduced written business; inconsistent pipeline creation |</p><p>| Chase/secure outstanding documents and update CRM blockers/next steps | Lloyd Dunlop | Paul (Adviser) | Not specified | Slippage; pipeline overstated; client disengagement |</p><p>| Request/track documents and progress to next stage post-offer accepted; update CRM | Mendon Dombrook | Paul (Adviser) | Not specified | Delay to application; client may go elsewhere |</p><p>| Increase DIP/AIP capture week-on-week and record clear next steps per lead | Lead pipeline (13 customers) | Paul (Adviser) | Not specified | Lower conversion/commitment; increased fallout risk |</p><p>| Provide complete application pack so admin can key quickly (fact-find + illustration + 3-year address history + liabilities) | Somer Hilo (Santander) + other apps | Paul (Adviser) | Not specified | Keying delays; rework; slower submission; adviser capacity squeezed |</p><p>| Key applications ad hoc once complete pack received | Somer Hilo (Santander) + other apps | Sean (Admin) | Not specified | Adviser time diverted; lead follow-up and revenue activity reduced |</p><p>---</p><p>## Email Recap (Auto-Generated From Review)</p><p>**Subject:** Weekly Performance Review and Agreed Actions</p><p>Hi Paul,</p><p>Below is a summary of this week’s review and agreed actions.</p><p>### Follow-Up From Last Week</p><p>**Completed**</p><p>- Debts reviewed/cleared. *(Owner: Paul)*</p><p>- Overdue tasks/workflows cleared. *(Owner: Paul)*</p><p>- Lead status clear-out completed on **2026-03-03**. *(Owner: Sean)*</p><p>**Outstanding / partially complete**</p><p>- Workflow handling: some workflows were moved forward without full client action/notes logged (risk: cases appear progressed without evidenced movement). *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: not specified)*</p><p>- DIP/AIP uplift remains required (explicit expectation: DIP count should rise week-on-week). *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: not specified)*</p><p>---</p><p>### Performance Snapshot (sections discussed)</p><p>**Key figures**</p><p>- February leads: **34 total** (**13 NPWS**, **7 keeping in contact**, **1 DIP**; largest category “unable to contact”).</p><p>- Leads view: **359** total; **66%** unable to contact/no-show; conversion **14% overall** and **54%** excluding uncontactables (**52 applications / 24 completed**).</p><p>- Pipeline: **£183,000**, noted as “everything’s offer”.</p><p>- Banked income: **~£10,000** in February; **~£30,000–£35,000** banked total stated.</p><p>- Projected income (where dates set): insurance **~£10,000**, admin fees **~£1,600**, mortgage **~£7,000** (March–April “well into bonus territory”).</p><p>- Admin fee rate: **56%** in Q4 vs **77%** YTD.</p><p>**What is working**</p><p>- Strong conversion when clients are engaged (**54%** excluding uncontactables).</p><p>- Strong pipeline progression to offer stage.</p><p>- Lead hygiene improving via Sean’s structured clear-outs.</p><p>**Identified risks**</p><p>- High uncontactable/no-show proportion (**66%**) suppressing overall conversion.</p><p>- CRM reliability risks from missing dates/status updates (e.g., missing on-risk dates; DIP completed but status not updated).</p><p>- Document delays causing case slippage (Lloyd Dunlop; Mendon Dombrook).</p><p>- Underwriting delays on Jasmine protection case.</p><p>---</p><p>### Cases Requiring Attention</p><p>- **Jacob &amp; Siobhan Duncan** — renewal; documents received; confirm appointment status and progress. *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: 2026-03-04)*</p><p>- **Brianna** — EWS1 uncertainty; review email, decide route (remortgage vs PT), log next steps. *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: 2026-03-04)*</p><p>- **Lloyd Dunlop** — waiting on documents. *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: not specified)*</p><p>- **Mendon Dombrook** — offer accepted; documents required to progress. *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: not specified)*</p><p>- **Jasmine (Protection)** — referral/underwriting delays; chase underwriting/provider/GPS and update workflow outcome. *(Owner: Sean; Deadline: 2026-03-04)*</p><p>- **David Lee** — missing on-risk date (projection not allocated to a month). *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: 2026-03-05)*</p><p>- **Jasmine Hepburn** — missing on-risk date (projection not allocated to a month). *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: 2026-03-05)*</p><p>- **Oliver Humphreys** — DIP completed but CRM lead status not updated. *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: 2026-03-05)*</p><p>- **Somer Hilo (Santander)** — send complete application pack for keying. *(Owner: Paul; Deadline: not specified)*</p><p>---</p><p>### Revenue and Pipeline Risks (discussed)</p><p>- Forecast accuracy risk due to missing on-risk dates (**David Lee**, **Jasmine Hepburn**).</p><p>- Admin fee discipline risk (Q4 **56%** vs **77%** YTD).</p><p>- Protection revenue slippage risk from underwriting/SLA delays (**Jasmine**) and escalation requirement (outstanding insurance case via Business Manager).</p><p>---</p><p>### Focus for Next Week</p><p>- Increase **DIP/AIP** progression week-on-week and record clear next steps per lead.</p><p>- Drive toward **3 quality appointments per day** and protect diary capacity to im